GazLang - a scripting language for web servers and command line tools
Usage: gaz [options] [file | -] [program arguments...]
  Runs the file, with everything after it as the program's arguments (args()).
  - reads the program from standard input, as no file does when something is piped.
  gaz -e 'code' [program arguments...]
  Runs the code given, with standard input left as the program's data (read_stdin(),
  read_line()) and everything after it as its arguments. Single-quote the code.
  gaz test [path...] [--update] [-v]
  Runs every *_test.gaz file under each path (default the current directory), recursively,
  printing the failures and a count per file; -v prints everything the files print.
Options:
  -h, --help     Show this help message
  -v, --version  Show version information
  -c, --code     Print the compiled bytecode instead of running it (gaz -c x.gaz > x.gzb)
  -t, --tokens   Print the lexer's tokens, one LINE TYPE VALUE per line, instead of interpreting
      --ast      Print the parser's tree instead of running it
  -f, --file     The file to run, as giving it first does
  -e, --eval     The code to run, in place of a file; given more than once, the pieces are lines
      --watch    Run the file, and run it again whenever it or a file it includes changes
      --tty[=COLSxROWS]
                 Run the file on a pretend terminal (120x40 unless given), to drive a program
                 made with std/tui without one: standard input is its keys, and each screen is
                 printed as numbered lines and the styles on them, when a 'snap' comes and at
                 the end. The keys are words, as term::name() writes them: j enter escape
                 ctrl+c page_up f5, "text to type", and 'wait 1.5' to let that many seconds
                 pass (the clock moves only then). gaz --tty app.gaz <<< "2 j snap enter"
  -S, --serve    Serve static files: gaz -S host:port [--docroot DIR] (see std/devserver.gaz)
